n my  design partner started in the Edit room  with Joe Walker from the beginning of  photography the idea being that  sound is such a credits 50% of the  motion-picture should inform the way the  Edit is created from day one it should  be constantly informing such that by the  time photography is completed you have a  rough idea of the kind of the the movie  that you have they you know they're  obviously roughing in temporary music  the composer  although he's very prescient that way  due he had brought on our composer  during product during the shoot and so  all sound and image were all informing  each other to create the Edit fact that  concept really came into fruition where  there was a scene in the casino with  Elvis and that whole part where it's all  cutting in and out that scene was almost  cut out of the movie it's one of the  best scenes in the movie and the reason  it's back in a main reason not the only  one but the first concept was to have it  so chaotic that all of those musical  elements were playing all at the same  time it was total chaos and they were  running around trying to shoot each  other you know getting fight and it was  so chaotic  you lost the fight you didn't know what  it was about so they scrapped the whole  scene it was gone and then the music  editor Clint Bennett and Theo his  partner took it apart and started  cutting things in and out and changing  it up and had great dynamics of stuff  it's kind of broken it's 30 years later  nothing's been repaired same kind of  idea and it was magic because it allowed  so much you know anticipation and he  didn't know what was coming next and it  was so chaotic but in a very dynamic way  and and it turned back into a chase and  a fight and that's what he loved and  that scene got back in the movie another  critical relationship is between myself  and the composer as well as with Ron  definitely yes ow especially on a film  like this where we intentionally tried  to cross borders we wanted this to be a  seamless soundtrack where you couldn't  tell where score left off and sound  design began and vice-versa so we have  to learn to work very closely with the  composer to know that we're working  harmoniously that that's a really  critical relationship there was a lot of  that going  in this film more than anything I think  I've done and the way it was presented  at the first place was to deliver a lot  of different stems of the music separate  parts so you would have one sense four  stems  yes stems are like broken out parts of  an entire mix so if you hear the whole  cue it would be the sum total of all  these parts so every track is separated  I would have one synth track a different  scent track a low thing a pad and all  these things are separate then we would  go through and listen to them all and  they were purposely over built so that  we could then sculpt and change things  depending on what they've got going the  sound design the sounds that Denis likes  or does not like and we would go through  and pick stuff with Danny and he would  say I like that I like that I really  like that but not in my movie and we  take that out and try this and say that  was we had a plethora of great stuff to  work with from Ben and Han so you can't  go wrong with that but Denis would strip  things down and make it very simplistic  and then sometimes we build it back up  and it allowed you to be able to push  and pull with when sound effects came  and went and marry them a lot more  organically overlapping and trading off  where normally you wouldn't be able to  if it was all glued together and you're  stuck like get rid of that we're only  playing this it wasn't that way in this  film at all he knows like most smart  directors that he didn't he gave me a  cut of the film with no music in it and  he edits with Joe Walker that way  because he wants to know that a scene  works on its own merits before the music  bumps it up to that next level so his  thought was on this film we could work  very musically very compositionally not  diegetic ly meaning not put uh sound  because I see something on camera he  wanted us to work in these long sweeping  arcs much more like a painting much more  like an abstract painting and so Thea  was offered also a composer and I both  had this opportunity to create musical  textures which is kind of what we  extrapolated from the first movie it's  one of the successes of the first film  is that in any given scene in Deckard's  apartment  or in or in tyrell corporation-- an  environment is informed or infused with  these musical sounds but it's not melody  it's not rhythm  it's an ambience it's a musical ambience  that gives a feel to a scene so we got  to bring to bear all of our musical  abilities and in creating those sound  compositions that underpin almost every  scene in our movie so musical ability  was very important I think everybody on  our crew is a musician yeah come to  think of it  Byron the dialog editors guitar player  my partner Doug another guitar player  everybody drummer Malone percussionist  but that came in handy there was a scene  the Vegas walk we call it when he's  walking through the Red Desert going to  the bees and then the casino if we  backup before that we were mixing away  and Marc called me at night and says the  legal department called says we can't  use anything from the original Blade  Runner so we had spinner sounds we had  these giant drum hits you know the big  bow you know and we had to take all  those out so he says can you rerecord  some of those at home tonight I have a  home studio in 7:1 and I was like sure  I'd love to got out all my drums I'm  banging all these drums till 3:00 in the  morning my wife loves that part and  mixed the whole thing and brought them  into this studio the next morning and  remastered them all and then we start  placing him throughout the film and we  put some in that scene as well well  there's a huge music queue that goes  through the whole thing was always  planned that way and they wrote a  beautiful cue then he started stripping  it out saying what if we make it less  and more minimalistic and take that out  and take that out I got to think I'm  like Denis all I have left are the drum  hits he goes play it so we hit play we  all sat back we're like wow it was so  cool because it was so dynamic and so  anticipatory like what's gonna happen  he's where's he going and it was just  really dramatic and so we kept it and  that's how that ended up like that there  throughout the whole film is the first  sound you hear in the mood  so I was very proud of that and just  love the sound of like this huge drum  down to like not fairly any win and just  a few footsteps I'm particularly proud  of that sequence because from the time  cabe walks in the desert till he meets  Deckard it's a twelve minute stretch of  cinema with no dialogue and no music  almost unheard of in cinema and that's  that's a sign of sort of deniz bravery  in his filmmaking that he thought that  the imagery and the sound could could  carry the weight of the film without  exposition or music he inherently  understands that when an audience enters  a movie theater part of our job is to  what they call suspend disbelief we know  a movie is an artifice and it's the  filmmakers job to create a world to  invite you into a world and get you to  sign off on it to believe in it so part  of the success of that weaving of Sound  of Music and having you wonder which was  which is that subconsciously  we saw it as one soundtrack it's you  know soundtrack you hear that term  soundtrack is them you buy it at the you  know at the store that's the music of  the movie but sound is all the sound  that you hear and we tried to create  something that was much more seamless  than that such that the audience would  never be thinking ah music cue sound  effect  we just wanted sound this is the sound  of the Bladerunner universe I thought it  was very successful for us in creating  something you'd never experienced before  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

